# Pre-restructure location of the downloaded NextGen spectra (~250 MB).  The
# live path is ignored above; this one is a leftover that make_bc wrote to
# before the models/ tree moved, and it is still populated in working trees
# that ran the SED tests.  Unignored, a `git add -A` in any worktree tries to
# commit a 247 MB file and GitHub rejects the push.
src/exozippy/components/sed/models/

# scripts/dump_code.py default output (a 2+ MB collated copy of the repo).
repo_dump.txt

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RUN ARTIFACTS.  `examples/*/fitresults/` above only ever covered the one
# directory name the early examples used; every real run since writes its
# output next to its config, under whatever directory the sweep chose
# (examples/DC2018/sweep/194/, ab194/av_true/, ...).  Left untracked-but-not-
# ignored, a single `git add -A` stages gigabytes -- one DC2018 trace is
# 4.4 GB and one corner plot is 48 MB -- and `git status` is unreadable.
# These are all products of a fit, reproducible from the config, the data and
# the commit; the config and the data stay tracked.
*_trace.nc
*_trace_detailed.pdf
*_corner.png
*_corner_*.png
*_lens_corner.png
*_results.csv
*_summary.txt
*_modes.txt
*_whitening.json
*_paper.tex
*_table.tex
*_definitions.tex
*_rejected_modes.tex
*_mmexofast.json
*.log

# mkparam's restart file, at any depth.  The rule above it
# (`/examples/*/*.params.*.yaml`) only matched one level down, so every
# sweep subdirectory's copy stayed untracked-but-not-ignored.  The INPUT
# `*.params.yaml` is deliberately NOT matched: that one is the fit's
# definition and belongs in the repo.
*.params.[0-9]*.yaml

# The LaTeX support files the paper writer copies in beside each run.
**/aastex*.cls
**/aasjournalv*.bst
**/references.bib
